 "status": null,
 "background": [
  {
   "text": "The stall is a condition where the angle of attack increases beyond a point such that the lift begins to decrease. Above AOAstall the airflow separates from the wing surface and the lift coefficient decreases.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCTM",
     "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
     "page": "P 23/32",
     "pdf": 468
    },
    {
     "book": "FCTM",
     "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
     "page": "P 24/32",
     "pdf": 469
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "The stall always occurs at the same angle of attack for a given configuration, Mach number and altitude. AOAstall depends on the aircraft configuration (slats, flaps, speed brakes), Mach and altitude, and wing contamination.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCTM",
     "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
     "page": "P 24/32",
     "pdf": 469
    },
    {
     "book": "FCTM",
     "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
     "page": "P 25/32",
     "pdf": 470
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "When the threshold is reached, permanent aural and visual alerts (\"STALL, STALL\" synthetic voice then cricket, \"STALL STALL\" red message on PFD) are triggered until a correct angle of attack is recovered.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCOM",
     "code": "PRO-ABN-NAV",
     "page": "P 123/124",
     "pdf": 3948
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Stall warnings give sufficient margin to alert the crew in advance of the actual stall, even with contaminated wings. Stall buffet is airframe vibration from the airflow separating when approaching AOAstall.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCTM",
     "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
     "page": "P 26/32",
     "pdf": 471
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "High altitude: buffeting increases due to the high Mach number, and both AOAstall and CL MAX decrease as Mach increases. For some Mach numbers the buffet may appear just before the stall warnings.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCTM",
     "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
     "page": "P 23/32",
     "pdf": 468
    },
    {
     "book": "FCTM",
     "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
     "page": "P 26/32",
     "pdf": 471
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Near the maximum altitude, margins reach their required minimum. Icing conditions or turbulence could rapidly lead to a slowdown, a buffet onset, a stall condition and, subsequently, a high altitude upset.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "PDP",
     "code": "Unreliable speed indication (and UPRT)",
     "page": "XFTDA6UNRASPD01",
     "pdf": 586
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "In alternate law the stall warnings are activated at an appropriate margin from the stall condition, the PFD speed scale shows a black/red barber pole below the stall warning, and the α floor protection is inoperative. In direct law, stall warnings are available as for alternate law.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCOM",
     "code": "DSC-27-20-20",
     "page": "P 12/20",
     "pdf": 1623
    },
    {
     "book": "FCOM",
     "code": "DSC-27-20-20",
     "page": "P 15/20",
     "pdf": 1626
    },
    {
     "book": "FCOM",
     "code": "DSC-27-20-20",
     "page": "P 17/20",
     "pdf": 1628
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "technique": [
  {
   "text": "The immediate key action is to reduce the angle of attack with a nose down pitch order on the sidestick: this ensures an immediate aircraft response.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCTM",
     "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
     "page": "P 26/32",
     "pdf": 471
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Minimizing the loss of altitude is secondary to reducing the angle of attack, as the first priority is to regain lift.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCTM",
     "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
     "page": "P 26/32",
     "pdf": 471
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Immediate maximum thrust on stall recognition is not appropriate. Engine spool up makes the speed increase slow, so it does not reduce the angle of attack instantaneously, and the thrust increase from under wing mounted engines generates a pitch up that may prevent the reduction.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCTM",
     "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
     "page": "P 26/32",
     "pdf": 471
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "When stall indications have stopped and the aircraft has recovered sufficient energy, smoothly recover the initial flight path.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCTM",
     "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
     "page": "P 26/32",
     "pdf": 471
    }
   ]
  },
  {
